The recursive formula for the sequence T(n) is:
T(n) = T(sqrt(n)) + 5n

To solve this using the telescoping method, we need to express the sum of terms from T(1) to T(n) as a single term. We do this by subtracting each term from the next term and adding it to the previous term:

T(n) - T(sqrt(n)) = 5n
T(sqrt(n)) - T(sqrt(sqrt(n))) = 5sqrt(n)
T(sqrt(sqrt(n))) - T(sqrt(sqrt(sqrt(n)))) = 5sqrt(sqrt(n))
...
T(1) - T(sqrt(sqrt(sqrt(sqrt(n)))} = 5\sqrt{n}

Now, we can add up all these differences to get the sum of the terms from T(1) to T(n):

T(n) - T(1) = (T(sqrt(n)) - T(sqrt(sqrt(n)))) + (T(sqrt(sqrt(n))) - T(sqrt(sqrt(sqrt(n))))) + ... + (T(\sqrt{n}) - T(\sqrt{n})) + 5\sqrt{n}
T(n) - T(1) = 5\sqrt{n} + 5\sqrt{n} + 5\sqrt{n} + ... + 5\sqrt{n} + 5\sqrt{n}
T(n) - T(1) = 5n\sqrt{n}

Therefore, the sum of the terms from T(1) to T(n) is given by:

T(n) = T(1) + 5n\sqrt{n}

Since T(1) = 0, we have:

T(n) = 5n\sqrt{n}

This is the solution to the recursive formula using the telescoping method.

The error you're encountering is a `java.util.InputMismatchException`. This exception is thrown when the input provided by the user does not match the expected data type. In this case, the error occurs when trying to read an integer using `Scanner.nextInt()` method.

To resolve this issue, you can check if the input provided by the user is valid before attempting to parse it as an integer. You can use the `hasNextInt()` method of the Scanner class to check if the next token in the input stream is an integer. If the input is not valid, you can display an error message and prompt the user to enter valid input.

Here's an example of how you can modify your code to handle this exception:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter the first number: ");
        while (!scanner.hasNextInt()) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter an integer.");
            scanner.next(); // Consume the invalid input
        }
        int num1 = scanner.nextInt();

        System.out.print("Enter the second number: ");
        while (!scanner.hasNextInt()) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter an integer.");
            scanner.next(); // Consume the invalid input
        }
        int num2 = scanner.nextInt();

        int sum = num1 + num2;
        System.out.println("The sum of " + num1 + " and " + num2 + " is " + sum);
    }
}
```

In this modified code, we use a while loop to check if the input provided by the user is valid. If the input is not valid, we display an error message and consume the invalid input using the `next()` method. Once the input is valid, we proceed with parsing it as an integer and performing the calculation.

This approach will help you handle input mismatch exceptions and ensure that your program runs smoothly even if the user provides invalid input.

