A JavaScript promise is a way to handle asynchronous operations, like fetching data from an API or loading a file. It's a way to make sure your code doesn't stop working while it's waiting for something else to finish.

Imagine you're trying to bake a cake. You need to preheat the oven, mix the ingredients, and then put the cake in the oven. While the cake is baking, you can't do anything else. You have to wait for the cake to be done before you can take it out and enjoy it.

That's kind of what happens when you're using asynchronous code. Your code can't keep running while it's waiting for something else to finish. That's where promises come in. They allow you to continue doing other things while you're waiting for something else to finish.

When you create a promise, you give it a function that will be called when the asynchronous operation is finished. The promise will then resolve or reject, depending on whether the operation was successful or not.

Here's an example:
```javascript
// Create a promise that will fetch some data from an API
const promise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  // Fetch the data from the API
  fetch('https://api.example.com/data')
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => resolve(data))
    .catch(error => reject(error));
});

// Use the promise to do something else while we're waiting for the data
console.log('Hello!');

// Once the data is fetched, the promise will resolve and we can use the data
promise.then(data => console.log('The data is:', data));
```
In this example, we create a promise that will fetch some data from an API. While we're waiting for the data, we can continue doing other things, like logging a message to the console. Once the data is fetched, the promise will resolve and we can use the data. If there's an error while fetching the data, the promise will reject and we can handle the error.

