```php
<?php
// Connect to the database
$servername = "localhost";
$username = "username";
$password = "password";
$dbname = "coffee_shop";

$conn = new mysqli($servername, $username, $password, $dbname);

// Check connection
if ($conn->connect_error) {
    die("Connection failed: " . $conn->connect_error);
}

// Query the database for loyal customers
$sql = "SELECT customer_id, COUNT(*) AS purchase_count
        FROM transactions
        WHERE product_id = 1 AND date >= DATE_SUB(CURDATE(), INTERVAL 7 DAY)
        GROUP BY customer_id
        HAVING purchase_count >= 3";

$result = $conn->query($sql);

// Display the results
if ($result->num_rows > 0) {
    echo "<table>";
    echo "<tr><th>Customer ID</th><th>Purchase Count</th></tr>";
    while($row = $result->fetch_assoc()) {
        echo "<tr><td>" . $row["customer_id"] . "</td><td>" . $row["purchase_count"] . "</td></tr>";
    }
    echo "</table>";
} else {
    echo "No loyal customers found.";
}

// Close the connection
$conn->close();
?>
```

This code assumes that you have a table named "transactions" with columns "customer_id", "product_id", and "date". The code filters the data to only include purchases of a specific product (product_id = 1) within the last week (DATE_SUB(CURDATE(), INTERVAL 7 DAY)). It then groups the data by customer_id and counts the number of purchases for each customer. Finally, it displays the results in an HTML table.

